@ The window procedure is called by Windows, outside the flow of our program, so the
little it has to communicate is left in global variables. A resize is not handled
immediately but recorded, and applied at a quiet moment of the main loop.

<<Global variables of the main program>>=
static App* g_app = nullptr;
static UINT g_resizeW = 0, g_resizeH = 0;

@ Messages go first to Dear ImGui, which needs the mouse and the keyboard; the rest are
the classic minimum of a Windows program. Swallowing |SC_KEYMENU| prevents the Alt key
from freezing the program in the (non-existent) window menu.

<<The window procedure>>=
static LRESULT CALLBACK WndProc(HWND hwnd, UINT msg, WPARAM wParam, LPARAM lParam) {
    if (ImGui_ImplWin32_WndProcHandler(hwnd, msg, wParam, lParam)) return true;
    switch (msg) {
    case WM_SIZE:
        if (wParam != SIZE_MINIMIZED) {
            g_resizeW = LOWORD(lParam);
            g_resizeH = HIWORD(lParam);
        }
        return 0;
    case WM_SYSCOMMAND:
        if ((wParam & 0xfff0) == SC_KEYMENU) return 0;  // no ALT application menu
        break;
    case WM_DESTROY:
        PostQuitMessage(0);
        return 0;
    }
    return DefWindowProcW(hwnd, msg, wParam, lParam);
}

